    wordCount: 302,
    text: `Detective Superintendent Claire Hargrove surveyed the disheveled study with the meticulous attention that twenty-three years of investigative work had instilled in her. The mahogany desk was undisturbed, a half-finished glass of Bordeaux standing precisely where its owner had apparently abandoned it. No signs of forced entry. No indication of a struggle. Lord Ashworth had simply vanished.

"The housekeeper discovered the room empty at approximately six-forty this morning," Sergeant Davies reported, consulting his notebook. "She says his lordship retired to the study around eleven last night, as was his invariable custom. The security system shows no doors or windows were breached between midnight and dawn."

Hargrove crouched beside the fireplace, examining the ashes with a penknife. Among the charred remnants of what appeared to be ordinary correspondence, she detected fragments of a thicker document—legal stationery, from the watermark. Someone had been burning contracts.

"Pull the financial records," she instructed. "Every transaction, every beneficiary, going back eighteen months. And find out who inherits this estate if Lord Ashworth is declared deceased."

The peculiar detail that nagged at her, however, was not the missing aristocrat or the incinerated documents. It was the dog. Lord Ashworth's elderly springer spaniel, renowned throughout the village for its fierce devotion to its master, had been found sleeping contentedly in the kitchen. The animal had not barked, had not scratched at doors, had exhibited no distress whatsoever.

Either the dog had been sedated—which the veterinary examination would confirm or eliminate within hours—or it had recognized whoever entered the study and felt no alarm. The implications narrowed the suspect pool considerably.

Hargrove straightened, her gaze settling on a barely perceptible scuff mark near the bookcase that lined the study's eastern wall. She pressed methodically along the shelving until she heard it—a faint mechanical click that resonated with the unmistakable promise of concealed passages and buried secrets.`,
